            <ArticleTitle>Acute red eye and back pain as a presentation for systemic illness: case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Acute red eye is a common presentation in both primary and secondary care. Presentation in combination with other systemic symptoms can indicate serious underlying pathology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE PRESENTATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">73-year-old lady presenting with endogenous endophthalmitis and thoracic discitis secondary to sub-acute bacterial endocarditis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Acute red eye in combination with systemic symptoms requires immediate investigation. If endogenous endophthalmitis is diagnosed, a source of sepsis should be comprehensively investigated and referral made to individual specialties if necessary.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
